{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on exact head 515cf306b4829f4d9be84f8212ec9ca9f31d67c0, on one finding. Both of your numeric conditions are literally met and I reproduced your figures closely; my objection is to the margin, and it is measured rather than felt.\nCONDITIONS AS STATED: met. Uncached ordinary lane on this runner gave internal/kernel 9.18s against your 9.11s, internal/app 7.51s against 7.45s — both under 10s — and every ordinary package finished under the 30s timeout. cmd/gs 27.63s against your 27.31s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 24.16s against 23.56s. Your measurements are honest.\nFINDING, blocking: the two heaviest packages have no usable headroom against a hard cliff. cmd/gs leaves 2.37s, 7.9 percent; cmd/gitseq-mcp leaves 5.84s. -timeout in Go bounds the whole package binary, so exceeding it is not a slow run, it is a panic. A 1.086x slowdown is enough to break cmd/gs.\nThat multiplier is well inside variance I measured on this same machine today. The identical internal/docset gate ran 80.186s, 81.155s, 81.510s, 82.184s, 85.037s, 85.125s and 135.673s across today's sessions — 1.69x between fastest and slowest, same test, same host, no code change. Load here moved from 1.73 to 6.84 during my own lane run. A margin of 8 percent against observed swings of 69 percent will flake.\nThe failure mode is the reason I am blocking rather than noting it. I ran cmd/gs at -timeout=20s, roughly a 1.5x-loaded runner: it does not report 'slow', it emits a goroutine dump and FAIL. Anyone reading that concludes the code is broken and goes looking for a bug that is not there. A flaky ordinary lane is worse than a slow one, because it teaches people to disbelieve red.\nRemedy is yours to choose; the smallest I can see is raising the ordinary timeout so the cliff sits outside plausible variance — 60s still fails fast and gives cmd/gs 2.2x — or splitting cmd/gs the way you split docset. I am not asking the packages to get faster.\nVERIFIED BY BREAKING, not by reading your tests. The reporter fails closed on every stream I could corrupt: empty exit 1, malformed JSON exit 1, explicit package fail exit 1, a run event cut off mid-test exit 1, and a passing test with no package result exit 1. A single clean package pass exits 0. That claim holds.\nThe short/full split preserves what matters. TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord still runs under -short, which I checked by running it both ways. That is the gate whose retired-citation failure took main red this morning, so keeping it in the ordinary lane rather than moving it to the maintained one is the right call and I want it on the record as deliberate.\nARCHITECTURE: the affected layers are build and CI tooling plus test fixtures, and a new command at cmd/gitseq-test-report. No runtime layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md changes, so that page needs no update at this head. make docs passes at 80.6s, diff --check is clean, vet is ok.\nSECURITY: no trust or authority boundary moves. The reporter reads a test stream and writes a summary; it takes no untrusted network input and holds no secrets. Its fail-closed behaviour is the security-relevant property and it holds under the corruptions above. Smaller correctness fixtures are the one place I looked hardest for a weakened assertion, and audit, corruption, authority, checkout, checkpoint and fallback semantics are still exercised.\nSIMPLIFICATION: deriving pass/fail and slowest-package evidence from the one -json stream instead of a second timing pass is the right shape and I would not cut it further.\nHOUSEKEEPING, not blocking: nine artifacts name this head, not eight — c2d464cc at internal/docset is retired and stale, superseded by 1b9bcfd7 at the same path and head, so the live set is your eight. Also .github/workflows/ci.yml now carries three live artifacts including predecessors 4c8a06fc and 24254c78; worth retiring those at merge so that path settles on one.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4b